WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:07.069 --> 00:00:08.610
TODD PREVIS: Good
morning, everybody.

00:00:08.610 --> 00:00:10.226
Thanks for coming.

00:00:10.226 --> 00:00:12.225
How many of you have a
visual effects background

00:00:12.225 --> 00:00:15.360
or are currently working
in visual effects?

00:00:15.360 --> 00:00:20.050
Cool, nice to see so many
of you here at GCP Next.

00:00:20.050 --> 00:00:21.520
And my name is Todd Previs.

00:00:21.520 --> 00:00:24.641
I'm a product manager on
the media team at Google.

00:00:24.641 --> 00:00:26.140
I've been at Google
about four years

00:00:26.140 --> 00:00:28.720
and I work on solutions
around content creation,

00:00:28.720 --> 00:00:30.082
so that's primarily rendering.

00:00:30.082 --> 00:00:31.540
And we're joined
by Steve Kawasaki.

00:00:31.540 --> 00:00:34.780
Steve is VP of Systems
Engineering at Sony Image

00:00:34.780 --> 00:00:35.740
Works.

00:00:35.740 --> 00:00:38.200
He's going to talk to you
about their pipelines,

00:00:38.200 --> 00:00:40.304
their experience using
Google Cloud Platform.

00:00:40.304 --> 00:00:42.220
And it's really all under
the guise of working

00:00:42.220 --> 00:00:44.430
with a hybrid render farm.

00:00:44.430 --> 00:00:45.900
As I'll talk to
you about in a bit,

00:00:45.900 --> 00:00:47.316
we've spent a lot
of time engaging

00:00:47.316 --> 00:00:49.240
with some of the
really large studios

00:00:49.240 --> 00:00:52.480
throughout the world for
visual effects rendering.

00:00:52.480 --> 00:00:55.510
And we found that all of them
have significant investment

00:00:55.510 --> 00:00:57.064
in their on-premise resources.

00:00:57.064 --> 00:00:58.480
So we've really
taken the approach

00:00:58.480 --> 00:01:01.510
of how do we offer an integrated
solution within Google Cloud

00:01:01.510 --> 00:01:02.260
Platform?

00:01:02.260 --> 00:01:05.680
What are the best practices to
enable those studios to utilize

00:01:05.680 --> 00:01:07.690
their existing
on-premise architecture,

00:01:07.690 --> 00:01:10.420
first and foremost, and
then burst to Google Cloud

00:01:10.420 --> 00:01:12.130
Platform as needed?

00:01:12.130 --> 00:01:14.620
So obviously all
of you are here,

00:01:14.620 --> 00:01:17.350
you're invested in
learning more about GCP,

00:01:17.350 --> 00:01:19.340
if you're not already using it.

00:01:19.340 --> 00:01:21.460
I don't need to go through
a lot of detail here.

00:01:21.460 --> 00:01:25.360
But the importance of Cloud as
a business to Google is massive.

00:01:25.360 --> 00:01:28.780
You can see the investments
we've made growing this.

00:01:28.780 --> 00:01:32.860
I remember my first GCP Next
four years ago was down 3rd

00:01:32.860 --> 00:01:36.400
Street, I believe that way,
much, much smaller crowd--

00:01:36.400 --> 00:01:39.430
and as to see the business
grow, see the interest grow,

00:01:39.430 --> 00:01:42.164
from customers and partners
all over the world--

00:01:42.164 --> 00:01:44.080
and even within Google
to see our organization

00:01:44.080 --> 00:01:45.330
grow has been really exciting.

00:01:45.330 --> 00:01:47.920
I'm now a veteran in my
fourth year of Google

00:01:47.920 --> 00:01:49.300
working on Cloud.

00:01:49.300 --> 00:01:51.400
What I think is of
importance specifically

00:01:51.400 --> 00:01:53.110
to note within
our cloud regions,

00:01:53.110 --> 00:01:56.200
where we actually
have compute capacity

00:01:56.200 --> 00:01:58.990
is our close proximity to
most of the major media

00:01:58.990 --> 00:02:01.670
content creation
hubs in the world.

00:02:01.670 --> 00:02:06.520
So think of the obvious places,
like London, L.A., Montreal,

00:02:06.520 --> 00:02:09.220
Oregon for folks up in Vancouver
doing a lot of feature film

00:02:09.220 --> 00:02:11.470
work, Sydney, Tokyo.

00:02:11.470 --> 00:02:14.020
So all of these major hubs
where a lot of content

00:02:14.020 --> 00:02:17.500
is created today have very
close proximity to Google Cloud

00:02:17.500 --> 00:02:19.400
Platform data centers.

00:02:19.400 --> 00:02:21.800
And this is useful for
a number of things.

00:02:21.800 --> 00:02:25.150
First of all, if you're doing
low latency NFS connectivity

00:02:25.150 --> 00:02:28.480
to mount render nodes, you can
do that in all these locations.

00:02:28.480 --> 00:02:31.280
We literally have a customer
in Los Angeles who is using

00:02:31.280 --> 00:02:32.530
our L.A. region for rendering.

00:02:32.530 --> 00:02:35.740
They have a, I believe
it's 1.4 millisecond ping

00:02:35.740 --> 00:02:39.100
from their facility in Santa
Monica to our L.A. data center.

00:02:39.100 --> 00:02:41.120
So literally when they
spin up render nodes

00:02:41.120 --> 00:02:43.810
it's as if those machines
are sitting in the room

00:02:43.810 --> 00:02:45.320
next to them.

00:02:45.320 --> 00:02:48.250
In addition, remote
workstations for artists

00:02:48.250 --> 00:02:51.430
who are working either remotely
in those cities or at those

00:02:51.430 --> 00:02:53.170
facilities is
something we can offer.

00:02:53.170 --> 00:02:55.280
We work with NVIDIA
on GPU offerings.

00:02:55.280 --> 00:02:57.100
We have a partnership
with Terdici

00:02:57.100 --> 00:03:00.412
for PC over IP and
their remote desktops.

00:03:00.412 --> 00:03:02.620
And actually, if you'd like
to learn more about that,

00:03:02.620 --> 00:03:05.110
on the third-- sorry, on the
second floor of the Moscone

00:03:05.110 --> 00:03:07.450
West here we have a complete
virtual studio set up

00:03:07.450 --> 00:03:10.269
where we have
artists on site here.

00:03:10.269 --> 00:03:11.560
We have artists in Los Angeles.

00:03:11.560 --> 00:03:13.420
And everyone's
working remotely off

00:03:13.420 --> 00:03:15.040
of workstations
running completely

00:03:15.040 --> 00:03:16.990
in the cloud on
Google Cloud Platform.

00:03:16.990 --> 00:03:19.156
Over the last few days
they've been putting together

00:03:19.156 --> 00:03:23.450
a 30 second animated piece using
products like Maya and Vray

00:03:23.450 --> 00:03:26.050
and Premier of Editorial,
courtesy of our partnership

00:03:26.050 --> 00:03:26.607
with Bebop.

00:03:26.607 --> 00:03:28.690
So they're putting together
this entire production

00:03:28.690 --> 00:03:31.630
live and in real time,
utilizing an infrastructure

00:03:31.630 --> 00:03:33.677
and resources provided by GCP.

00:03:33.677 --> 00:03:35.510
So I think when you
take a look at this map,

00:03:35.510 --> 00:03:38.980
take a look at the locations
where we have data centers

00:03:38.980 --> 00:03:41.740
and understand again
the importance of media

00:03:41.740 --> 00:03:45.544
and the media vertical to Google
Cloud Platform as a whole.

00:03:45.544 --> 00:03:47.960
And when you talk about Google
Cloud Platform in our media

00:03:47.960 --> 00:03:49.376
solutions--
obviously, we're going

00:03:49.376 --> 00:03:51.507
to focus on rendering and
hybrid rendering today.

00:03:51.507 --> 00:03:53.090
But it is important
to note that we're

00:03:53.090 --> 00:03:56.300
looking to solve the
ecosystem problem--

00:03:56.300 --> 00:03:58.200
or provide the
ecosystem solution

00:03:58.200 --> 00:04:00.220
I should say, really
from glass to glass.

00:04:00.220 --> 00:04:04.040
So all the way from your content
acquisition when you're on set,

00:04:04.040 --> 00:04:06.530
all the way through to your
final output distribution

00:04:06.530 --> 00:04:10.076
utilizing platforms such as
Chromecast and Daydream VR.

00:04:10.076 --> 00:04:11.450
Imagine when you're
on set you're

00:04:11.450 --> 00:04:13.550
collecting terabytes
and terabytes of data,

00:04:13.550 --> 00:04:15.950
how do you get all that data
quickly up to the cloud?

00:04:15.950 --> 00:04:17.866
I mean, we have a number
of different options.

00:04:17.866 --> 00:04:19.820
We have something such
as partner interconnect

00:04:19.820 --> 00:04:22.370
or private interconnect
that allows you a dedicated

00:04:22.370 --> 00:04:23.600
connection to our network.

00:04:23.600 --> 00:04:25.100
We also have a
transfer appliance,

00:04:25.100 --> 00:04:26.975
a physical appliance,
where you can literally

00:04:26.975 --> 00:04:30.290
load up to 480 terabytes
onto that and ship that to us

00:04:30.290 --> 00:04:32.660
and get it on Google
Cloud Platform-- and all

00:04:32.660 --> 00:04:33.860
of the solutions in between.

00:04:33.860 --> 00:04:35.526
And obviously not all
of you are working

00:04:35.526 --> 00:04:38.270
in all of the different stages
in the production pipeline,

00:04:38.270 --> 00:04:40.700
but we feel as though through
our software as a service

00:04:40.700 --> 00:04:43.610
solutions, our infrastructure,
and our partnerships

00:04:43.610 --> 00:04:46.880
with leading providers of tools
that people are using today,

00:04:46.880 --> 00:04:50.270
we really do aim to provide
the entire end-to-end suite

00:04:50.270 --> 00:04:53.300
of solutions in media
and content creation

00:04:53.300 --> 00:04:54.740
all the way through
distribution.

00:04:54.740 --> 00:04:57.440
And of course, that's augmented
by a monetization platform,

00:04:57.440 --> 00:04:59.450
by things we're doing with ML.

00:04:59.450 --> 00:05:02.150
So again, in media,
it's critical to us

00:05:02.150 --> 00:05:05.670
to offer the end-to-end
platform solutions.

00:05:05.670 --> 00:05:07.070
Since this is a
rendering talk, I

00:05:07.070 --> 00:05:09.140
do want to speak very
briefly about ZYNC Render.

00:05:09.140 --> 00:05:10.810
It's something very
near and dear to me.

00:05:10.810 --> 00:05:12.435
I've been working on
it for seven years

00:05:12.435 --> 00:05:14.930
and was actually involved in
it before we joined Google.

00:05:14.930 --> 00:05:17.360
It is our software as a
service, completely managed

00:05:17.360 --> 00:05:18.230
rendering solution.

00:05:18.230 --> 00:05:21.440
And it's really aimed at
small to medium size studios.

00:05:21.440 --> 00:05:23.600
So what we have is
plug-ins that sit

00:05:23.600 --> 00:05:26.900
inside most of the major
content creation applications.

00:05:26.900 --> 00:05:28.550
And the idea is for
studios that don't

00:05:28.550 --> 00:05:31.754
have necessarily the development
operations to set up a cloud

00:05:31.754 --> 00:05:33.920
render farm or to manage
licensing or infrastructure

00:05:33.920 --> 00:05:35.574
or asset synchronization--

00:05:35.574 --> 00:05:37.490
ZYNC really takes care
of all of that for you.

00:05:37.490 --> 00:05:39.770
It's quite an auto magical
solution we like to say.

00:05:39.770 --> 00:05:42.770
So literally you can
be inside of Maya

00:05:42.770 --> 00:05:44.510
using Arnold or
VRay or Renderman,

00:05:44.510 --> 00:05:47.482
want to fire up a render, simply
click on the ZYNC plug-in.

00:05:47.482 --> 00:05:49.190
It connects you to
Google Cloud Platform.

00:05:49.190 --> 00:05:53.525
You can spin up to 48,000 render
nodes, complete with licensing,

00:05:53.525 --> 00:05:55.730
all of the asset
synchronization,

00:05:55.730 --> 00:05:58.619
and your render is taking
care of things for you.

00:05:58.619 --> 00:06:00.660
We've been working on ZYNC
for quite a while now,

00:06:00.660 --> 00:06:02.534
so we've tried to build
more and more complex

00:06:02.534 --> 00:06:06.020
tools into it, in terms of
job predictability over cost,

00:06:06.020 --> 00:06:08.150
in terms of management
for your files,

00:06:08.150 --> 00:06:10.700
in terms of permissions
over how many users

00:06:10.700 --> 00:06:15.380
can access which projects, how
many cores, how many resources.

00:06:15.380 --> 00:06:17.550
So we are continuing
to develop this.

00:06:17.550 --> 00:06:19.980
We've had some really
great progress with this.

00:06:19.980 --> 00:06:22.130
We've had actually
an Academy Award

00:06:22.130 --> 00:06:24.430
nominated film, which I
can't go into details yet,

00:06:24.430 --> 00:06:26.130
actually use ZYNC for rendering.

00:06:26.130 --> 00:06:29.780
So we feel like we've been
able to enable small to medium

00:06:29.780 --> 00:06:32.330
studios to really leverage
the power of cloud,

00:06:32.330 --> 00:06:35.570
even if they don't necessarily
have the development resources

00:06:35.570 --> 00:06:37.670
that the larger
studios might have.

00:06:37.670 --> 00:06:40.100
I think it's also important
to note how we've strengthened

00:06:40.100 --> 00:06:42.350
our relationships with
most of the leading ISVs

00:06:42.350 --> 00:06:45.082
out there in the content
creation space, which initially

00:06:45.082 --> 00:06:46.790
came from some of the
ZYNC relationships.

00:06:46.790 --> 00:06:48.860
So if you're using
ZYNC now for rendering,

00:06:48.860 --> 00:06:51.460
you can see we support
most of the major ISVs,

00:06:51.460 --> 00:06:56.900
so products like Maya eds Max,
Renderman, Arnold, VRay, Cinema

00:06:56.900 --> 00:07:00.420
4D, Houdini, and Nuke
from the Foundry.

00:07:00.420 --> 00:07:02.930
We've also been able to
expand on these relationships

00:07:02.930 --> 00:07:04.700
with several of these partners.

00:07:04.700 --> 00:07:07.490
In the case of KS Group,
there Vray cloud product,

00:07:07.490 --> 00:07:08.992
which is a plug-in--

00:07:08.992 --> 00:07:10.700
sorry, a service that
sits inside of VRay

00:07:10.700 --> 00:07:13.370
next allowing people to
leverage cloud for rendering

00:07:13.370 --> 00:07:15.590
is actually running on
Google Cloud Platform.

00:07:15.590 --> 00:07:17.270
And the Foundry's
Athera Product,

00:07:17.270 --> 00:07:19.610
which is their studio
in the cloud offering

00:07:19.610 --> 00:07:21.650
the complete suite
of foundry tools,

00:07:21.650 --> 00:07:23.760
as well as some additional
third party tools

00:07:23.760 --> 00:07:25.070
is also running on GCP.

00:07:25.070 --> 00:07:27.770
And that's a particularly
interesting product in the fact

00:07:27.770 --> 00:07:30.290
that its again designed for
small to medium studios that

00:07:30.290 --> 00:07:33.140
want to literally spin
up a complete workstation

00:07:33.140 --> 00:07:35.347
environment, but
they can leverage GCP

00:07:35.347 --> 00:07:36.930
and it's a completely
managed service.

00:07:36.930 --> 00:07:39.500
So all the studio needs,
or all the artist needs,

00:07:39.500 --> 00:07:41.785
is a laptop or a
desktop thin client

00:07:41.785 --> 00:07:43.160
and they can
literally be working

00:07:43.160 --> 00:07:45.980
with Nuke or Moto or Gatana
in a matter of moments,

00:07:45.980 --> 00:07:49.270
all powered by GCP.

00:07:49.270 --> 00:07:51.850
And as you'll hear from
Steve, obviously, GCP

00:07:51.850 --> 00:07:54.070
has been working wonders
at Sony Image Works.

00:07:54.070 --> 00:07:56.650
We've also worked with
a lot of large studios

00:07:56.650 --> 00:07:58.275
throughout the world.

00:07:58.275 --> 00:08:00.400
I'm sure a lot of these
names look familiar to you,

00:08:00.400 --> 00:08:02.900
but places like
Frame Store and MPC.

00:08:02.900 --> 00:08:06.820
Zoik Studios-- we had the CTO
of Zoik speaking a few days ago

00:08:06.820 --> 00:08:10.030
at one of our leadership circle
events, talking about some

00:08:10.030 --> 00:08:11.630
of the work that
they've been doing.

00:08:11.630 --> 00:08:13.982
So the infrastructure
as a service offering

00:08:13.982 --> 00:08:15.940
from Google Cloud Platform
and our relationship

00:08:15.940 --> 00:08:19.380
with these really large studios
that are running exclusively

00:08:19.380 --> 00:08:21.940
in a multiclass-- sorry,
in a hybrid environment

00:08:21.940 --> 00:08:25.850
between on premise and cloud
is also really important to us.

00:08:25.850 --> 00:08:29.080
And so why is hybrid rendering
important to studios and why

00:08:29.080 --> 00:08:30.180
is it important to us?

00:08:30.180 --> 00:08:32.140
I tried to break
it down into kind

00:08:32.140 --> 00:08:34.480
of the reality of
what's going on today

00:08:34.480 --> 00:08:36.520
and what we see the future as.

00:08:36.520 --> 00:08:39.022
And I think first and
foremost, it is the reality

00:08:39.022 --> 00:08:40.480
that all of these
studios have made

00:08:40.480 --> 00:08:42.770
significant on-premise
investments.

00:08:42.770 --> 00:08:45.462
And those investments are going
to stay for the next three

00:08:45.462 --> 00:08:47.920
to five, maybe seven years,
depending on how long they want

00:08:47.920 --> 00:08:49.390
to sweat those machines out.

00:08:49.390 --> 00:08:51.466
But it's not a short
gain where everyone

00:08:51.466 --> 00:08:52.840
is going to go
ahead and take all

00:08:52.840 --> 00:08:54.714
of their on-premise
resources get rid of them

00:08:54.714 --> 00:08:56.140
and spin everything
over to cloud.

00:08:56.140 --> 00:08:58.056
So again, it's how do
we work with the studios

00:08:58.056 --> 00:08:59.890
to provide a hybrid
environment that

00:08:59.890 --> 00:09:02.350
allows them to utilize their
on-premise resources when

00:09:02.350 --> 00:09:05.650
it's appropriate, and then
burst to cloud beyond that?

00:09:05.650 --> 00:09:07.750
I think the reality of it
also comes down somewhat

00:09:07.750 --> 00:09:09.166
to cost considerations
when you're

00:09:09.166 --> 00:09:10.960
talking about really
large visual effects

00:09:10.960 --> 00:09:14.950
animation studios that have
projects planned for 12 to 18

00:09:14.950 --> 00:09:19.310
to 24 months and they're
running at 90%, 95% utilization.

00:09:19.310 --> 00:09:21.940
The metrics in terms of their
cost on-premise and cloud

00:09:21.940 --> 00:09:24.220
is probably still somewhat
slightly less expensive

00:09:24.220 --> 00:09:25.327
to run on-premise first.

00:09:25.327 --> 00:09:27.910
Again, but we understand that
we want to work with the studios

00:09:27.910 --> 00:09:30.220
to help them to enable more
and more of their workload

00:09:30.220 --> 00:09:32.970
over to GCP over time.

00:09:32.970 --> 00:09:34.515
Anytime you've got
a large studio,

00:09:34.515 --> 00:09:36.640
oftentimes you're talking
about multiple locations,

00:09:36.640 --> 00:09:39.310
so having a unified pipeline
management is critical.

00:09:39.310 --> 00:09:41.380
If you want one queuing
system that manages your

00:09:41.380 --> 00:09:44.274
on premise resources, that
manages your cloud resources,

00:09:44.274 --> 00:09:45.940
you want a single
machine image that you

00:09:45.940 --> 00:09:49.060
can run on-premise
[? docorize ?] that, put that

00:09:49.060 --> 00:09:52.060
on GCP, and not have to
manage separate versions when

00:09:52.060 --> 00:09:54.970
you're running on-prem
versus running on cloud.

00:09:54.970 --> 00:09:56.950
So it really does come
down to burst capacity,

00:09:56.950 --> 00:09:58.866
and Steve's going to
share with you some stats

00:09:58.866 --> 00:10:00.850
and data on how
Sony sees the cloud

00:10:00.850 --> 00:10:02.770
and how they've
used us for burst.

00:10:02.770 --> 00:10:05.310
And ultimately we do see this
as the gateway to the future.

00:10:05.310 --> 00:10:07.268
And I'm not talking about
the next two or three

00:10:07.268 --> 00:10:09.730
years where everyone's going
in all cloud all the time.

00:10:09.730 --> 00:10:12.160
You know, but imagine a
future where literally you

00:10:12.160 --> 00:10:14.620
have access to endless
compute resources

00:10:14.620 --> 00:10:17.500
anywhere in the world, whether
it's for your remote desktops,

00:10:17.500 --> 00:10:19.477
whether it's for
your render farm.

00:10:19.477 --> 00:10:21.310
And think of the fact
that you can literally

00:10:21.310 --> 00:10:24.320
spin up a studio
anywhere in the world,

00:10:24.320 --> 00:10:27.520
whether it be for tax purposes
or for other business reasons,

00:10:27.520 --> 00:10:29.810
and not have to invest in
set up and infrastructure.

00:10:29.810 --> 00:10:31.227
So it's still the
very early days,

00:10:31.227 --> 00:10:33.184
but I think we've started
to really demonstrate

00:10:33.184 --> 00:10:34.870
the value of cloud
in working with a lot

00:10:34.870 --> 00:10:36.820
of these large studios.

00:10:36.820 --> 00:10:39.340
And so in terms of Google
as an infrastructure

00:10:39.340 --> 00:10:42.100
provider for visual
effects animation

00:10:42.100 --> 00:10:45.480
rendering, in particular,
whether we feel is strongest.

00:10:45.480 --> 00:10:48.130
And I just want to highlight
a few of the things

00:10:48.130 --> 00:10:50.670
at our fundamental
offering level

00:10:50.670 --> 00:10:53.830
that studios have shared with
us is really critical in terms

00:10:53.830 --> 00:10:55.310
of doing their rendering work.

00:10:55.310 --> 00:10:57.760
Compute obviously, its
kind of the baseline core

00:10:57.760 --> 00:10:59.260
when it comes to rendering.

00:10:59.260 --> 00:11:01.801
And I want to call it the fact
that we have a set of machines

00:11:01.801 --> 00:11:04.612
called premptable VMs or PMS
as they're mainly referred to,

00:11:04.612 --> 00:11:06.070
and that's probably
what I'll refer

00:11:06.070 --> 00:11:07.660
to them the rest of this talk.

00:11:07.660 --> 00:11:10.940
These are essentially
spare capacity machines.

00:11:10.940 --> 00:11:12.760
And these are available
at an 80% discount

00:11:12.760 --> 00:11:14.530
of our on-demand machines.

00:11:14.530 --> 00:11:16.565
These machines can be
taken away if capacity

00:11:16.565 --> 00:11:18.190
is required from
another customer who's

00:11:18.190 --> 00:11:19.990
using them at full price.

00:11:19.990 --> 00:11:22.120
But I think for fault
tolerant workloads

00:11:22.120 --> 00:11:23.710
it's really the
machine of choice

00:11:23.710 --> 00:11:25.780
for people doing rendering.

00:11:25.780 --> 00:11:27.430
One of the great
things we've seen out

00:11:27.430 --> 00:11:29.888
of most of the major renders
out there-- again, when you're

00:11:29.888 --> 00:11:31.870
talking about Arnold,
VRay, and Renderman--

00:11:31.870 --> 00:11:33.911
is the fact that they all
have some sort of check

00:11:33.911 --> 00:11:36.260
pointing or snapshotting
mechanism built in now.

00:11:36.260 --> 00:11:37.840
So the fact is you
can set that up.

00:11:37.840 --> 00:11:40.360
You can run a render and right
the partially completed frame

00:11:40.360 --> 00:11:41.500
back to disc.

00:11:41.500 --> 00:11:44.380
So in the event you're running a
machine for four or five hours,

00:11:44.380 --> 00:11:46.090
let's say you've got
half the frame done

00:11:46.090 --> 00:11:47.589
and that machine
is preempted, well,

00:11:47.589 --> 00:11:50.200
if you enabled snecked
snapshotting or checkpointing,

00:11:50.200 --> 00:11:52.840
you can simply pull up
another premptable VM

00:11:52.840 --> 00:11:53.942
and resume the renders.

00:11:53.942 --> 00:11:55.150
You haven't lost all of that.

00:11:55.150 --> 00:11:57.770
So for fault tolerant
workloads, such as rendering,

00:11:57.770 --> 00:11:59.720
we have really seen
that premptable VMs

00:11:59.720 --> 00:12:02.530
are the machine of choice.

00:12:02.530 --> 00:12:04.810
We also offer
custom VMs, so this

00:12:04.810 --> 00:12:08.350
allows studios to exactly tweak
the VM size for the kind of job

00:12:08.350 --> 00:12:09.320
they need.

00:12:09.320 --> 00:12:13.600
You can go up to 96
cores for you your CPUs

00:12:13.600 --> 00:12:16.210
and I believe it's over
a terabyte now for ram.

00:12:16.210 --> 00:12:18.516
So if you're doing a very
multithreaded heavy Arnold

00:12:18.516 --> 00:12:20.140
or VRay or Remnderman
job, you probably

00:12:20.140 --> 00:12:21.797
want to crank to 96 cores.

00:12:21.797 --> 00:12:24.130
If you're doing something
like a comp job in Nuke that's

00:12:24.130 --> 00:12:27.110
not threading quite as high,
maybe 16 cores is adequate.

00:12:27.110 --> 00:12:30.250
So we give customers and
studios the flexibility

00:12:30.250 --> 00:12:34.120
to actually configure VM
to whatever they need.

00:12:34.120 --> 00:12:35.980
When I say massive
size, I'm simply

00:12:35.980 --> 00:12:39.670
talking about the scale of
our data centers worldwide.

00:12:39.670 --> 00:12:42.250
We have a customer
called Atomic Fiction.

00:12:42.250 --> 00:12:44.500
I've been working with them
for about seven years now.

00:12:44.500 --> 00:12:46.430
They were actually a ZYNC
customer many years ago.

00:12:46.430 --> 00:12:47.940
And they're called
founder Ryan Todehope

00:12:47.940 --> 00:12:49.620
I think has a great
quote that I like

00:12:49.620 --> 00:12:51.540
to use-- you can have
an IMX sized render

00:12:51.540 --> 00:12:55.230
farm at one minute and a IOM
sized render farm the next.

00:12:55.230 --> 00:12:57.780
So it's really that ability
to spin up and scale

00:12:57.780 --> 00:13:02.400
your requirements on-demand
with literally no notice.

00:13:02.400 --> 00:13:05.520
That I think makes cloud
and makes our capacity

00:13:05.520 --> 00:13:07.680
quite attractive to the
large studios looking

00:13:07.680 --> 00:13:09.102
for burst capacity.

00:13:09.102 --> 00:13:10.560
In terms of latest
and greatest, we

00:13:10.560 --> 00:13:12.182
do offer the Skylake machines.

00:13:12.182 --> 00:13:14.140
And those machines can
be spun up very quickly.

00:13:14.140 --> 00:13:15.431
So it's a very fast deployment.

00:13:15.431 --> 00:13:17.040
You're looking at
30 seconds or so

00:13:17.040 --> 00:13:19.730
from when you actually boot up
a VM to when it comes online.

00:13:19.730 --> 00:13:22.530
And so along with our compute,
we now have GPUs from NVIDIA.

00:13:22.530 --> 00:13:27.630
We have the KADs, the P100s, the
V100s that just announced P4s.

00:13:27.630 --> 00:13:30.990
And all of these can be used
in a very flexible manner,

00:13:30.990 --> 00:13:32.040
similar to our CPUs.

00:13:32.040 --> 00:13:33.810
So you get bare
metal performance.

00:13:33.810 --> 00:13:36.725
You can attach multiple GPU
accounts to any instance.

00:13:36.725 --> 00:13:38.100
And depending on
the kind of work

00:13:38.100 --> 00:13:39.558
you're doing-- as
I was mentioning,

00:13:39.558 --> 00:13:41.194
we have a wide variety of GPUs.

00:13:41.194 --> 00:13:43.110
And again, I would really
encourage any of you

00:13:43.110 --> 00:13:45.090
who are interested in kind
of the remote workstation

00:13:45.090 --> 00:13:47.650
visualization side of things,
just go down to two after this.

00:13:47.650 --> 00:13:49.358
We have a nice meta
entertainment section

00:13:49.358 --> 00:13:50.450
in the solutions area.

00:13:50.450 --> 00:13:52.950
And you'll literally see-- we
have three or four artists now

00:13:52.950 --> 00:13:54.430
working all remotely.

00:13:54.430 --> 00:13:56.940
We're using NVIDIA
GPUs and Teridici

00:13:56.940 --> 00:14:00.990
to run virtual workstations
in creating their content.

00:14:00.990 --> 00:14:03.550
Storage is obviously
a very important part,

00:14:03.550 --> 00:14:05.880
especially if you're not
doing an NFS mount to a data

00:14:05.880 --> 00:14:07.110
center for close proximity.

00:14:07.110 --> 00:14:10.890
So if you're using our Iowa data
center, for example, from L.A.

00:14:10.890 --> 00:14:12.750
or from Vancouver,
it's important to note

00:14:12.750 --> 00:14:14.880
we have a variety of
different storage options.

00:14:14.880 --> 00:14:16.950
And of course, much of
the rendering software

00:14:16.950 --> 00:14:19.817
today is legacy and does
require a POSIX interface.

00:14:19.817 --> 00:14:21.150
So we have all sorts of things--

00:14:21.150 --> 00:14:22.410
I'm not going to go
of the entire list.

00:14:22.410 --> 00:14:24.960
But if you need caching there
are products that could be

00:14:24.960 --> 00:14:26.450
[? there ?] [? that ?]
picks cache.

00:14:26.450 --> 00:14:28.200
Cloud file storage
that recently announced

00:14:28.200 --> 00:14:31.050
managed service for NFS Filer
and a number of other options

00:14:31.050 --> 00:14:32.730
in there as well.

00:14:32.730 --> 00:14:35.580
Our network-- I'm sure if you've
been to any other sessions

00:14:35.580 --> 00:14:39.110
today or this week you've
heard our network called out.

00:14:39.110 --> 00:14:40.650
But it is the largest
cloud network.

00:14:40.650 --> 00:14:43.110
And it's the fiber that
we've owned and we've laid.

00:14:43.110 --> 00:14:46.320
And we've heard certainly
from large studios that

00:14:46.320 --> 00:14:48.390
have locations
throughout the world,

00:14:48.390 --> 00:14:50.940
being able to pass data once
they connected to our network

00:14:50.940 --> 00:14:53.790
is just incredibly reliable
and incredibly high speed.

00:14:53.790 --> 00:14:56.430
So think of studios that have
offices in London, Mumbai,

00:14:56.430 --> 00:14:59.130
Vancouver, Los Angeles that
are now delivering shows

00:14:59.130 --> 00:15:03.100
in six to eight months when they
used to have 12 to 24 months.

00:15:03.100 --> 00:15:05.100
The cycles to deliver
are compressing.

00:15:05.100 --> 00:15:07.350
And the fact that you need
essentially these almost 24

00:15:07.350 --> 00:15:11.550
hour work times to have
multiple locations working

00:15:11.550 --> 00:15:14.650
on a single shot is something
that our network provides

00:15:14.650 --> 00:15:16.970
great flexibility with.

00:15:16.970 --> 00:15:19.050
And now I'm not going to
go too much into this,

00:15:19.050 --> 00:15:20.550
but I think we're
really starting

00:15:20.550 --> 00:15:23.190
to see some of our customers--
especially when it comes

00:15:23.190 --> 00:15:25.140
to visual effects
content creation-- take

00:15:25.140 --> 00:15:27.420
a deep dive in machine
learning and really want

00:15:27.420 --> 00:15:29.670
to understand, what
sort of predictability

00:15:29.670 --> 00:15:30.810
can I have with Cloud?

00:15:30.810 --> 00:15:32.670
It tends to be one
of the major blockers

00:15:32.670 --> 00:15:34.670
when you first start
engaging with the studio.

00:15:34.670 --> 00:15:36.045
Like, well we
don't know how much

00:15:36.045 --> 00:15:38.796
this is going to cost, how
much time it's going to take.

00:15:38.796 --> 00:15:40.170
And what we've
seen more and more

00:15:40.170 --> 00:15:43.170
are customers starting to look
at the data from past jobs

00:15:43.170 --> 00:15:45.840
and try to understand and put
together models as to actually

00:15:45.840 --> 00:15:47.749
what is the cost for
this particular shot.

00:15:47.749 --> 00:15:49.290
What is the cost
for this time frame?

00:15:49.290 --> 00:15:50.970
And I think as we
start to see more

00:15:50.970 --> 00:15:53.390
and more of these solutions
around ML come online,

00:15:53.390 --> 00:15:56.110
it's going to alleviate a
significant amount of kind

00:15:56.110 --> 00:16:01.350
of the doubt and concern when it
comes to the unknown for cloud.

00:16:01.350 --> 00:16:03.600
I want to wrap up and talk
a little bit about security

00:16:03.600 --> 00:16:04.910
because it really is critical.

00:16:04.910 --> 00:16:07.350
And we were among the
earliest cloud providers

00:16:07.350 --> 00:16:10.560
to tackle this issue,
specifically for a feature

00:16:10.560 --> 00:16:12.910
film, tier 1 content
I like to say.

00:16:12.910 --> 00:16:16.410
So what we've done is work
with all of the major Hollywood

00:16:16.410 --> 00:16:19.690
studios in terms of
working for approval

00:16:19.690 --> 00:16:23.040
regards to allowing their
most sensitive content to be

00:16:23.040 --> 00:16:24.610
run on GCP.

00:16:24.610 --> 00:16:26.470
So what we do is a
quarterly audit now

00:16:26.470 --> 00:16:28.210
with independent
security evaluators.

00:16:28.210 --> 00:16:29.850
They're known as ISE.

00:16:29.850 --> 00:16:32.700
And they are one of
the cloud auditors

00:16:32.700 --> 00:16:33.810
for Disney, in particular.

00:16:33.810 --> 00:16:35.726
And if any of you have
worked in feature film,

00:16:35.726 --> 00:16:38.550
you know that certainly the
standards required by Disney

00:16:38.550 --> 00:16:40.800
around cloud security,
around on-premise security

00:16:40.800 --> 00:16:42.990
are quite high.

00:16:42.990 --> 00:16:44.220
They are doing film--

00:16:44.220 --> 00:16:48.570
Marvel shows, Star Wars,
multibillion dollar films

00:16:48.570 --> 00:16:51.204
and making sure that
content is protected

00:16:51.204 --> 00:16:52.620
throughout the
pre-release process

00:16:52.620 --> 00:16:54.310
is really critical to them.

00:16:54.310 --> 00:16:58.110
So what we undergo is a
quarterly audit of GCP with ISE

00:16:58.110 --> 00:17:00.720
and that again is done
specifically for Disney.

00:17:00.720 --> 00:17:04.530
We have a redacted audit
report that we share from ISE

00:17:04.530 --> 00:17:06.329
with all of the major studios.

00:17:06.329 --> 00:17:08.650
So we work through content
protection requirements

00:17:08.650 --> 00:17:10.200
with all of those studios.

00:17:10.200 --> 00:17:13.319
We've actually drafted a
securing rendering workloads

00:17:13.319 --> 00:17:15.630
solution, which has been
published on our website.

00:17:15.630 --> 00:17:19.230
And this has been approved
and vetted by these studios.

00:17:19.230 --> 00:17:21.990
Which essentially is a
step-by-step, very prescriptive

00:17:21.990 --> 00:17:25.349
guideline as to how you,
as a visual effects vendor,

00:17:25.349 --> 00:17:29.220
can connect securely to
Google Cloud Platform

00:17:29.220 --> 00:17:31.260
when you're running
your rendering workload.

00:17:31.260 --> 00:17:33.630
So again, on top of
all the standard audits

00:17:33.630 --> 00:17:37.170
we do across the
cloud for Google,

00:17:37.170 --> 00:17:40.200
media entertainment security and
auditing, specifically working

00:17:40.200 --> 00:17:42.890
with the big studios, is
really critical to us.

00:17:42.890 --> 00:17:46.080
And what we will be working
with in the near future

00:17:46.080 --> 00:17:47.400
is the trusted partner network.

00:17:47.400 --> 00:17:49.590
And this is going to be
more of a formal consortium

00:17:49.590 --> 00:17:53.190
among the major studios, among
several other major content

00:17:53.190 --> 00:17:55.440
providers in media.

00:17:55.440 --> 00:17:57.420
I believe it's among
the MPA and the CDSA.

00:17:57.420 --> 00:18:00.610
And it's going to be one
standard audit that essentially

00:18:00.610 --> 00:18:04.230
is similar to what we do now
in terms of the requirements.

00:18:04.230 --> 00:18:07.092
But it's going to be more of
a formalized version of that,

00:18:07.092 --> 00:18:09.300
that [? assets ?] Google
Cloud Platform can say, yes,

00:18:09.300 --> 00:18:12.760
we've undergone this trusted
partner network audit for cloud

00:18:12.760 --> 00:18:14.930
and now have approval
from the studios.

00:18:14.930 --> 00:18:17.392
So that's about it
for me, right on time.

00:18:17.392 --> 00:18:19.350
I'm going to turn over
to Steve Kowalski, who's

00:18:19.350 --> 00:18:21.266
going to share his
thoughts on the Image Works

00:18:21.266 --> 00:18:22.800
pipeline running on GCP.

00:18:22.800 --> 00:18:23.676
Thanks Steve.

00:18:23.676 --> 00:18:24.176
Thank you.

00:18:24.176 --> 00:18:26.566
[CLAPPING]

00:18:30.390 --> 00:18:33.780
STEVE KOWALSKI: OK, well, thanks
for everyone coming out today.

00:18:33.780 --> 00:18:36.720
I'm going to apologize
up front for what

00:18:36.720 --> 00:18:39.530
may seem like a fairly
lengthy lead in,

00:18:39.530 --> 00:18:44.100
but I want to try and frame
the problem that we're solving

00:18:44.100 --> 00:18:45.610
by using the Google Cloud.

00:18:45.610 --> 00:18:50.020
And I'm going to stick
to my script on this

00:18:50.020 --> 00:18:51.520
because there's a
lot to get through

00:18:51.520 --> 00:18:53.520
and I want to try and
leave some time at the end

00:18:53.520 --> 00:18:57.130
for any questions
that you might have.

00:18:57.130 --> 00:19:02.880
And lastly, I completely forgot
about San Francisco coffee

00:19:02.880 --> 00:19:05.190
and I had a cup of
coffee this morning,

00:19:05.190 --> 00:19:09.210
and I'm surprised I
didn't jog to the arena

00:19:09.210 --> 00:19:12.840
this morning because it's
really got me amped up.

00:19:12.840 --> 00:19:22.250
So best I stick to
the script, so OK,

00:19:22.250 --> 00:19:23.540
so my name's Steve Kowalski.

00:19:23.540 --> 00:19:26.300
As Todd said, I run
systems engineering

00:19:26.300 --> 00:19:28.670
for Sony Pictures Image Works.

00:19:28.670 --> 00:19:32.930
I've been at Image Works
for about 23 years now.

00:19:32.930 --> 00:19:37.550
And I've held just about
every technical position

00:19:37.550 --> 00:19:40.250
within systems
engineering, all the way up

00:19:40.250 --> 00:19:41.330
to running the group.

00:19:41.330 --> 00:19:44.960
I have responsibility
over the entire technical

00:19:44.960 --> 00:19:46.100
infrastructure.

00:19:46.100 --> 00:19:49.670
And also have responsibility
at our sister company, Sony

00:19:49.670 --> 00:19:51.440
Pictures Animation.

00:19:51.440 --> 00:19:55.920
In addition to that, I'm a
Sony distinguished engineer.

00:19:55.920 --> 00:20:00.930
We've been working with Google
for about eight years now.

00:20:00.930 --> 00:20:02.710
We're GCP customers.

00:20:02.710 --> 00:20:04.610
Were also G Suite customers.

00:20:04.610 --> 00:20:08.820
And that's how we first came
into contact with one another.

00:20:08.820 --> 00:20:12.560
We've been talking to Google
about rendering in the cloud

00:20:12.560 --> 00:20:17.150
since before GCP
was even a product

00:20:17.150 --> 00:20:22.880
offering from Google, so a
really long time at this point.

00:20:22.880 --> 00:20:24.770
So Image Works is
a little unique

00:20:24.770 --> 00:20:27.560
within the post-production
space in that it simultaneously

00:20:27.560 --> 00:20:30.680
works on both visual effects
projects in full length

00:20:30.680 --> 00:20:34.560
animated features using the
same production pipeline.

00:20:34.560 --> 00:20:38.510
So typically you work on one
or the other, but not both.

00:20:38.510 --> 00:20:41.070
And that's what makes us unique.

00:20:41.070 --> 00:20:43.400
We've been in business
for over 25 years,

00:20:43.400 --> 00:20:45.050
have worked with
all the major motion

00:20:45.050 --> 00:20:49.130
picture studios on well over
100 projects at this point.

00:20:49.130 --> 00:20:53.240
We're headquartered in
British Columbia in Vancouver,

00:20:53.240 --> 00:20:57.410
have an office in Culver City,
and run a large data center

00:20:57.410 --> 00:20:59.790
in Quincy, Washington.

00:20:59.790 --> 00:21:02.780
At peak, we grow to
about 1,300 artists,

00:21:02.780 --> 00:21:05.270
operate tens of thousands
of rendering cores,

00:21:05.270 --> 00:21:08.435
and manage multiple
petabytes of storage.

00:21:10.960 --> 00:21:14.520
So here's a high level picture
of our wide area network.

00:21:14.520 --> 00:21:16.290
We run multiple
10 gig wave length

00:21:16.290 --> 00:21:20.280
services that connects Seattle,
Vancouver, Quincy, and Culver

00:21:20.280 --> 00:21:21.270
City.

00:21:21.270 --> 00:21:25.154
We operate our own NPLS network
to provide both redundancy

00:21:25.154 --> 00:21:25.820
and flexibility.

00:21:25.820 --> 00:21:30.540
So you can think of
this as an NPLS cloud.

00:21:30.540 --> 00:21:34.410
We maintain points of presence
in Vancouver, Seattle,

00:21:34.410 --> 00:21:36.000
and Los Angeles.

00:21:36.000 --> 00:21:38.490
And we currently peer
with Google in Seattle

00:21:38.490 --> 00:21:42.570
to reduce the network latency
back to our Quincy data center.

00:21:42.570 --> 00:21:44.580
We're also working
on establishing

00:21:44.580 --> 00:21:47.745
another peering relationship
in downtown Los Angeles

00:21:47.745 --> 00:21:51.510
so that we can take advantage
of the new Los Angeles data

00:21:51.510 --> 00:21:54.110
center.

00:21:54.110 --> 00:21:57.380
All our projects are
big and are fixed fee.

00:21:57.380 --> 00:21:59.810
Our workforce tends to
be ephemeral and scales

00:21:59.810 --> 00:22:02.990
up and down in sync with the
cyclical and seasonal nature

00:22:02.990 --> 00:22:04.240
of our business.

00:22:04.240 --> 00:22:07.940
By cyclical, I mean the times
of year such as this summer

00:22:07.940 --> 00:22:10.420
and the key holiday periods
in which large [? tam ?]

00:22:10.420 --> 00:22:14.090
[? pull ?] feature films
are typically released.

00:22:14.090 --> 00:22:19.920
Ultimately, these seasons are
what drive our computing needs.

00:22:19.920 --> 00:22:22.350
Recently, our schedule
has become much more

00:22:22.350 --> 00:22:27.000
aggressive to allow filmmakers
as much flexibility as possible

00:22:27.000 --> 00:22:29.160
to make the very best pictures.

00:22:29.160 --> 00:22:33.700
Unfortunately, shorter
schedules means less time.

00:22:33.700 --> 00:22:36.320
Less time means we
need more people.

00:22:36.320 --> 00:22:39.030
And more people means
we need more resources.

00:22:39.030 --> 00:22:41.784
Often projects allow
such little time

00:22:41.784 --> 00:22:43.200
that the only way
to get them done

00:22:43.200 --> 00:22:44.570
is through sheer brute force.

00:22:47.520 --> 00:22:49.960
So many different types
of artists work on a movie

00:22:49.960 --> 00:22:53.570
and each follow the same
basic iterative process.

00:22:53.570 --> 00:22:55.670
An artist uses
specialized software

00:22:55.670 --> 00:22:57.320
to create a script
of instructions

00:22:57.320 --> 00:23:00.770
for creating an output, submits
that script to the render farm

00:23:00.770 --> 00:23:03.500
to compute the output, and
then presents the output

00:23:03.500 --> 00:23:05.330
to the director for feedback.

00:23:05.330 --> 00:23:08.060
The director's feedback
informs the next cycle

00:23:08.060 --> 00:23:14.160
until the director decides
the output is just right.

00:23:14.160 --> 00:23:16.350
A movie is made up of
scenes, and scenes are

00:23:16.350 --> 00:23:18.990
composed of constituent shots.

00:23:18.990 --> 00:23:22.440
Groups of artists, each working
in different disciplines,

00:23:22.440 --> 00:23:26.460
such as modeling, layout,
effects, layering, et cetera,

00:23:26.460 --> 00:23:30.930
make the necessary ingredients
used to assemble our shots.

00:23:30.930 --> 00:23:33.440
Each artist working
in each discipline

00:23:33.440 --> 00:23:35.520
is using outputs
from other artists

00:23:35.520 --> 00:23:38.640
to create iterations
of their own work.

00:23:38.640 --> 00:23:41.310
For example, a downstream
artist in lighting

00:23:41.310 --> 00:23:43.170
can't do their job
without layouts.

00:23:43.170 --> 00:23:46.830
So inputs take it from
layout, modeling, rigging,

00:23:46.830 --> 00:23:47.880
and animation.

00:23:47.880 --> 00:23:53.250
This process plays out over
time until the shot's complete.

00:23:53.250 --> 00:23:54.980
So why go to cloud?

00:23:54.980 --> 00:23:58.490
Well for us, what had
been the typical project

00:23:58.490 --> 00:24:00.890
is really no longer typical.

00:24:00.890 --> 00:24:04.130
And worse, we can no
longer count on projects

00:24:04.130 --> 00:24:06.040
to start when they should.

00:24:06.040 --> 00:24:09.360
And more often than not, they
all run at the same time.

00:24:09.360 --> 00:24:12.770
So let's take a look at that.

00:24:12.770 --> 00:24:15.370
So not all artist disciplines
have the same resource

00:24:15.370 --> 00:24:16.300
requirements.

00:24:16.300 --> 00:24:19.870
Those who come in towards the
tail end of the process, namely

00:24:19.870 --> 00:24:21.880
those doing color
and lighting work,

00:24:21.880 --> 00:24:24.760
tend to have the heaviest
resource needs and the least

00:24:24.760 --> 00:24:27.710
amount of time to
complete the work.

00:24:27.710 --> 00:24:30.550
So from a resource
standpoint, every project

00:24:30.550 --> 00:24:32.650
becomes back-ended.

00:24:32.650 --> 00:24:35.290
Just how back-ended a
project becomes is influenced

00:24:35.290 --> 00:24:38.560
by a number of variables,
including script rewrites,

00:24:38.560 --> 00:24:42.670
character environment
changes, indecision--

00:24:42.670 --> 00:24:44.170
you get the point.

00:24:44.170 --> 00:24:48.200
Almost always though, the
delivery date never moves.

00:24:48.200 --> 00:24:50.350
So any of these changes
result in more work

00:24:50.350 --> 00:24:52.680
to complete in a
shorter amount of time,

00:24:52.680 --> 00:24:56.130
and something has to give.

00:24:56.130 --> 00:24:58.860
In a perfect world, you
work on multiple projects

00:24:58.860 --> 00:25:00.540
and they time out
nicely so you get

00:25:00.540 --> 00:25:02.410
good utilization
of your render farm

00:25:02.410 --> 00:25:05.410
and maintain a
steady artist base.

00:25:05.410 --> 00:25:09.000
Here we look at three features
running in the same year,

00:25:09.000 --> 00:25:11.430
each spread out in
a manageable way.

00:25:11.430 --> 00:25:14.220
In a world like this you can
buy the rendering cores you need

00:25:14.220 --> 00:25:15.760
and it's a good investment.

00:25:15.760 --> 00:25:19.540
And this is kind of what Todd
was referring to earlier.

00:25:19.540 --> 00:25:22.630
Lately though, these
have not been so ideal

00:25:22.630 --> 00:25:24.430
and projects are
piling up on one

00:25:24.430 --> 00:25:26.480
another creating
hiring challenges

00:25:26.480 --> 00:25:28.600
and being resource shortfalls.

00:25:28.600 --> 00:25:30.340
And this is before
anything unexpected

00:25:30.340 --> 00:25:32.650
happens on one or more
of the projects, which

00:25:32.650 --> 00:25:35.710
is something you can
certainly count on happening.

00:25:35.710 --> 00:25:40.040
This has become the new
normal within entertainment.

00:25:40.040 --> 00:25:41.960
So where does the cloud come in?

00:25:41.960 --> 00:25:44.070
Well there's really
two parts to this.

00:25:44.070 --> 00:25:46.880
One's defensive and
the other offensive.

00:25:46.880 --> 00:25:49.700
On the defensive side,
are resource deficits

00:25:49.700 --> 00:25:52.640
have become so large that
traditional procurement

00:25:52.640 --> 00:25:55.010
is simply too expensive.

00:25:55.010 --> 00:25:58.100
These shows require upwards
of $10 million or more

00:25:58.100 --> 00:26:00.500
and additional
resources for periods

00:26:00.500 --> 00:26:04.520
as short as four to six weeks
in order to finish the project.

00:26:04.520 --> 00:26:07.280
At this scale, few
companies are willing and/or

00:26:07.280 --> 00:26:12.050
able to rent bare metal servers
for such short periods of time.

00:26:12.050 --> 00:26:13.760
Again, we're talking
a few weeks here.

00:26:13.760 --> 00:26:15.920
Anyone who's willing
to rent wants

00:26:15.920 --> 00:26:18.280
to rent for multiple
years, not weeks,

00:26:18.280 --> 00:26:21.930
and the price is
generally very expensive.

00:26:21.930 --> 00:26:24.090
The on-demand burst
model of cloud

00:26:24.090 --> 00:26:28.080
fits with our sporadic capacity
and flexibility requirements

00:26:28.080 --> 00:26:31.710
without us having to
make any commitment.

00:26:31.710 --> 00:26:35.180
Our projects change
literally from day to day.

00:26:35.180 --> 00:26:38.890
So that flexibility is
really important to us.

00:26:38.890 --> 00:26:41.380
Lastly with cloud, there's
no physical infrastructure

00:26:41.380 --> 00:26:45.670
to manage, which cuts down on
the ancillary costs associated

00:26:45.670 --> 00:26:52.010
with logistics, installation,
and ongoing operation.

00:26:52.010 --> 00:26:54.860
On the offensive end is speed.

00:26:54.860 --> 00:26:57.230
Looking back at the
artist cycle, the quicker

00:26:57.230 --> 00:27:00.560
we cycle, the fewer
artists we need,

00:27:00.560 --> 00:27:05.390
or the more work we can take in,
or the more iterations we get.

00:27:05.390 --> 00:27:08.420
And we might even
get a better picture.

00:27:08.420 --> 00:27:11.210
We also feel strongly that our
infrastructure should never

00:27:11.210 --> 00:27:12.890
be the slowest link.

00:27:12.890 --> 00:27:16.590
Its actually the one area
we have complete control.

00:27:16.590 --> 00:27:19.580
And if can scale up resources
to save artist time,

00:27:19.580 --> 00:27:22.940
or give our clients what
they want when they want it,

00:27:22.940 --> 00:27:25.840
we'll gladly do so.

00:27:25.840 --> 00:27:28.800
So why use the Google
Cloud Platform?

00:27:28.800 --> 00:27:31.110
Well we've tried other
platforms at scale

00:27:31.110 --> 00:27:33.010
and we've found
success using them,

00:27:33.010 --> 00:27:37.380
but for us Google sticks
out in a few ways.

00:27:37.380 --> 00:27:40.320
A key differentiator for us
is Google's highly performant

00:27:40.320 --> 00:27:42.300
network and infrastructure.

00:27:42.300 --> 00:27:45.810
Google really likes to
point this out to customers,

00:27:45.810 --> 00:27:47.790
as Todd did earlier.

00:27:47.790 --> 00:27:50.820
I always thought, of course
they're going to say this.

00:27:50.820 --> 00:27:52.810
Why wouldn't they say this?

00:27:52.810 --> 00:27:56.250
But it really wasn't until we
ran it scale and had experience

00:27:56.250 --> 00:27:58.230
using other platforms
that we truly

00:27:58.230 --> 00:27:59.580
understood what they meant.

00:27:59.580 --> 00:28:02.350
And it turns out
that they were right.

00:28:02.350 --> 00:28:06.510
Google's PVMs yams fit well
into our rendering methodology.

00:28:06.510 --> 00:28:10.460
We launched an N1 standard
16 or an N1 standard 32

00:28:10.460 --> 00:28:12.840
PVM per frame.

00:28:12.840 --> 00:28:14.850
And all machines are
the same, meaning

00:28:14.850 --> 00:28:16.740
we don't have to
take what's available

00:28:16.740 --> 00:28:19.290
or manage complicated
bidding strategies.

00:28:19.290 --> 00:28:21.880
We choose what we
want, when we want.

00:28:21.880 --> 00:28:24.660
And if we ever need something
custom we request it,

00:28:24.660 --> 00:28:27.210
and we pay a little bit more.

00:28:27.210 --> 00:28:30.660
The Google Cloud is simple,
straightforward, and for us,

00:28:30.660 --> 00:28:33.960
has proved to cost as
much as 50% less than

00:28:33.960 --> 00:28:36.490
other alternatives
in the market.

00:28:36.490 --> 00:28:39.550
Lastly, the Google team
understands our business

00:28:39.550 --> 00:28:41.830
because the network of
support they've built

00:28:41.830 --> 00:28:44.570
is made up of people who
come from our business.

00:28:44.570 --> 00:28:47.680
And that's really
important to us.

00:28:47.680 --> 00:28:52.060
So let's take a closer
look at our GCP pipeline.

00:28:52.060 --> 00:28:53.850
Today our GCP
pipeline is modeled

00:28:53.850 --> 00:28:57.160
to support on-demand
burst rendering.

00:28:57.160 --> 00:28:58.000
That's it.

00:28:58.000 --> 00:28:59.340
That's all we do.

00:28:59.340 --> 00:29:02.760
To keep costs low,
we use only PVMs.

00:29:02.760 --> 00:29:06.960
We cache all data using
Avere vFXT clusters.

00:29:06.960 --> 00:29:09.780
Are instances spin up
in under 30 seconds

00:29:09.780 --> 00:29:12.090
and are generally available
in our render queue

00:29:12.090 --> 00:29:14.380
in under two minutes.

00:29:14.380 --> 00:29:18.420
When we're preempted,
and we are preempted,

00:29:18.420 --> 00:29:21.970
we checkpoint our rendering
data in under 30 seconds.

00:29:21.970 --> 00:29:26.380
We run only Arnold renders and
typically run 16 to 32 threads.

00:29:26.380 --> 00:29:29.500
So we're simply doing
ray tracing here.

00:29:29.500 --> 00:29:31.540
Instances are
treated as ephemeral

00:29:31.540 --> 00:29:34.030
with all effort
built into the image,

00:29:34.030 --> 00:29:36.310
rather than in post-processing.

00:29:36.310 --> 00:29:38.530
And we mostly launched
the N1 standard

00:29:38.530 --> 00:29:45.070
16 instances, which provides 16
vCPUs and 60 gigabytes of RAM.

00:29:45.070 --> 00:29:47.670
Now here's a diagram of
what this looks like.

00:29:47.670 --> 00:29:50.500
Q3 servers at SPI
work in conjunction

00:29:50.500 --> 00:29:54.745
with the provisioning system to
launch PVMs and GCP that read

00:29:54.745 --> 00:29:57.850
and write data through
Avere virtual caches.

00:29:57.850 --> 00:29:59.900
For management
and orchestration,

00:29:59.900 --> 00:30:03.020
a Bastian host is
set up within GCP,

00:30:03.020 --> 00:30:06.070
so we prevent our admins
from running G Cloud commands

00:30:06.070 --> 00:30:08.200
on SPI machines.

00:30:08.200 --> 00:30:11.560
Logs are sent to BigQuery so
that we can analyze use later

00:30:11.560 --> 00:30:13.580
for billing purposes.

00:30:13.580 --> 00:30:17.020
And a link between SPI and
GCP is a private 10 gig

00:30:17.020 --> 00:30:18.970
interconnect with VPN.

00:30:18.970 --> 00:30:20.705
And again, for us,
that's in Seattle.

00:30:23.650 --> 00:30:27.640
Our first burst rendering
project was the "Emoji Movie".

00:30:27.640 --> 00:30:32.410
We started the project on AWS,
and we finished it using GCP.

00:30:32.410 --> 00:30:36.040
We ran for about
three weeks in GCP.

00:30:36.040 --> 00:30:39.330
"Emoji" was the first time
we ran in the cloud at scale,

00:30:39.330 --> 00:30:42.990
and we ended up growing much
larger than anyone had planned.

00:30:42.990 --> 00:30:46.290
We ended up growing to be about
5x what we initially thought

00:30:46.290 --> 00:30:48.060
we would run going into it.

00:30:48.060 --> 00:30:51.240
So as a result we
were really unprepared

00:30:51.240 --> 00:30:53.980
and we learned a whole
lot from that experience.

00:30:53.980 --> 00:30:57.120
And there are really four
main takeaways for us.

00:30:57.120 --> 00:31:00.690
First, it was very difficult
to tie the GCP auto

00:31:00.690 --> 00:31:03.090
scaler with our
queuing system and we

00:31:03.090 --> 00:31:05.850
made some real costly mistakes.

00:31:05.850 --> 00:31:08.850
So we quickly took over
the necessary orchestration

00:31:08.850 --> 00:31:11.640
to scale down when we
needed to, and that really

00:31:11.640 --> 00:31:13.970
helped us curb runaway costs.

00:31:13.970 --> 00:31:17.160
So you can imagine
going into a weekend

00:31:17.160 --> 00:31:19.410
and your queue
clears out, and then

00:31:19.410 --> 00:31:21.810
your host keep running
until Monday morning

00:31:21.810 --> 00:31:22.870
and you pay for that.

00:31:22.870 --> 00:31:26.016
So that's a costly error.

00:31:26.016 --> 00:31:27.390
Second, we learned
that we needed

00:31:27.390 --> 00:31:30.570
robust logging and statistics
collection so we always

00:31:30.570 --> 00:31:33.360
knew what was
running versus what

00:31:33.360 --> 00:31:35.400
the queue thought was running.

00:31:35.400 --> 00:31:38.970
There were times when many more
instances were running then

00:31:38.970 --> 00:31:39.990
were in our queue.

00:31:39.990 --> 00:31:43.260
Again, this became costly,
so we wrote scripts

00:31:43.260 --> 00:31:46.950
to collect data and
pull our system.

00:31:46.950 --> 00:31:49.320
Third, it became
clear that we could

00:31:49.320 --> 00:31:52.680
manage cloud hosts like we
manage bare metal systems.

00:31:52.680 --> 00:31:56.100
Heavy handed routine
management simply

00:31:56.100 --> 00:32:00.870
led to longer launch times,
increased failure, and larger

00:32:00.870 --> 00:32:03.900
than acceptable numbers of
instances failing to make it

00:32:03.900 --> 00:32:06.420
to our queuing system.

00:32:06.420 --> 00:32:10.442
So despite all these challenges,
it was also abundantly clear

00:32:10.442 --> 00:32:12.150
that this was going
to be the way forward

00:32:12.150 --> 00:32:13.860
for all our future needs.

00:32:13.860 --> 00:32:17.700
Using cloud on "Emoji"
literally saved the project.

00:32:17.700 --> 00:32:19.800
We were able to double
our rendering capacity

00:32:19.800 --> 00:32:23.370
for a period of five weeks
and increase productivity

00:32:23.370 --> 00:32:26.980
of some artists groups
by as much as 2x,

00:32:26.980 --> 00:32:27.855
which that was huge.

00:32:30.980 --> 00:32:33.410
Eight months later we
were burst rendering again

00:32:33.410 --> 00:32:35.920
to finish "Hotel
Transylvania III".

00:32:35.920 --> 00:32:39.740
In all, we ran for
about six weeks in GCP.

00:32:39.740 --> 00:32:41.930
"Hotel TIII" was
the first show where

00:32:41.930 --> 00:32:45.290
we managed all orchestration
of our instances,

00:32:45.290 --> 00:32:48.140
scaling them up and down
using our own scripts.

00:32:48.140 --> 00:32:50.270
It's also the first
show where we strictly

00:32:50.270 --> 00:32:52.790
enforced managing our
image, rather than

00:32:52.790 --> 00:32:57.290
the individual instances
using Puppet and Young.

00:32:57.290 --> 00:33:00.050
Less management translated
to quicker launch times

00:33:00.050 --> 00:33:01.730
with less failure.

00:33:01.730 --> 00:33:05.720
We had to keep telling
ourselves these are PVMs.

00:33:05.720 --> 00:33:07.070
They're ephemeral.

00:33:07.070 --> 00:33:09.960
And in the best case, they're
going to live 24 hours.

00:33:09.960 --> 00:33:13.370
So there's no need to manage
them once they're launched.

00:33:13.370 --> 00:33:15.950
But old habits can be
really hard to break.

00:33:15.950 --> 00:33:19.130
This took a lot of
work on our part.

00:33:19.130 --> 00:33:22.160
So I think there are
four main differences

00:33:22.160 --> 00:33:24.800
that I can highlight
from "The Emoji Movie".

00:33:24.800 --> 00:33:30.370
The first is that we use the N1
standard 16 instances versus N1

00:33:30.370 --> 00:33:33.080
high CPU16 instances.

00:33:33.080 --> 00:33:36.530
It turned out on this project
we needed the extra memory

00:33:36.530 --> 00:33:39.830
and we were OK paying a
little bit more for that.

00:33:39.830 --> 00:33:43.070
Second, no bin
packing was allowed.

00:33:43.070 --> 00:33:46.010
We ran a single job per
instance and threaded

00:33:46.010 --> 00:33:49.210
over all available cores.

00:33:49.210 --> 00:33:51.700
This gave us the option
to kill the instance

00:33:51.700 --> 00:33:53.140
once the job finished.

00:33:53.140 --> 00:33:56.530
Again, we were aiming for a
little more efficiency here.

00:33:56.530 --> 00:33:59.170
Third, as mentioned, we
introduced our own scripts

00:33:59.170 --> 00:34:01.300
to scale up and
down automatically,

00:34:01.300 --> 00:34:03.670
abandoning the GCP auto scaler.

00:34:03.670 --> 00:34:06.160
And lastly, as I
just also mentioned,

00:34:06.160 --> 00:34:09.100
we managed our image
rather than our instances,

00:34:09.100 --> 00:34:14.199
reducing error, and
improving overall efficiency.

00:34:14.199 --> 00:34:17.179
The [? our ?] experience
cloudbursting on "Hotel TIII"

00:34:17.179 --> 00:34:20.989
turned out to be very different
from "The Emoji Movie".

00:34:20.989 --> 00:34:23.210
We introduced a new
rendering workflow

00:34:23.210 --> 00:34:25.429
that combined persistent
check pointing

00:34:25.429 --> 00:34:27.650
with a custom de-noiser
to cut our frame

00:34:27.650 --> 00:34:30.860
times by as much as 45%.

00:34:30.860 --> 00:34:33.860
As a result, our
instance group size

00:34:33.860 --> 00:34:38.030
would often fluctuate wildly
depending on the time of day.

00:34:38.030 --> 00:34:44.570
And you can see peak times
for us were typically

00:34:44.570 --> 00:34:47.989
right before lunch and
right before people

00:34:47.989 --> 00:34:50.090
would go home at night.

00:34:50.090 --> 00:34:52.040
And I should note
here too on this chart

00:34:52.040 --> 00:34:54.560
that we're measuring cores.

00:34:54.560 --> 00:34:58.381
And for us a core is
equivalent to two vCPUs.

00:34:58.381 --> 00:34:58.880
Right?

00:34:58.880 --> 00:35:01.880
So but here, you see a
nice sawtooth pattern.

00:35:01.880 --> 00:35:03.620
And the queue loads
up with frames

00:35:03.620 --> 00:35:06.530
and empties out in
a matter of hours.

00:35:06.530 --> 00:35:08.930
I should also note
that during "Hotel T"

00:35:08.930 --> 00:35:12.150
we no longer regulated how
quickly we scaled our instance

00:35:12.150 --> 00:35:13.340
groups.

00:35:13.340 --> 00:35:15.440
So you can see
clearly in the chart--

00:35:15.440 --> 00:35:19.190
maybe you can-- at
6 PM on May 9th,

00:35:19.190 --> 00:35:23.450
where we go from 6,000 cores to
over 20,000 cores in one move.

00:35:23.450 --> 00:35:25.880
Those all launched
in under two minutes.

00:35:25.880 --> 00:35:27.995
This is really,
really powerful stuff.

00:35:32.494 --> 00:35:34.910
So here's a breakdown of where
the costs landed on a show.

00:35:34.910 --> 00:35:37.130
Three quarters of
our total spend

00:35:37.130 --> 00:35:40.150
was allocated directly
to processing,

00:35:40.150 --> 00:35:42.620
and that sort of felt
about right to us.

00:35:42.620 --> 00:35:46.970
That comports with [? maybe ?]
our traditional capital

00:35:46.970 --> 00:35:50.080
spending on infrastructure,
so it seems right.

00:35:52.710 --> 00:35:56.640
So today, right
now, at this moment,

00:35:56.640 --> 00:35:59.350
we're burst rendering on a
project called "Smallfoot".

00:35:59.350 --> 00:36:02.160
And even though we're using the
same rendering optimizations

00:36:02.160 --> 00:36:05.100
introduced on "Hotel T",
our "Smallfoot" experience

00:36:05.100 --> 00:36:07.820
seems more akin to
"The Emoji Movie".

00:36:07.820 --> 00:36:10.380
That's because "Smallfoot"
has much heavier scenes

00:36:10.380 --> 00:36:15.240
than did "Hotel Transylvania"
and the 45% efficiency gain

00:36:15.240 --> 00:36:18.030
simply brings our frame
times into alignment

00:36:18.030 --> 00:36:20.140
with our historical trend.

00:36:20.140 --> 00:36:21.570
So there's one big
change, though.

00:36:21.570 --> 00:36:24.000
The N1 standard
16th instance type

00:36:24.000 --> 00:36:26.920
lacks sufficient memory
for about half the jobs

00:36:26.920 --> 00:36:28.660
we wanted to run.

00:36:28.660 --> 00:36:31.020
So we launched
another instance group

00:36:31.020 --> 00:36:34.050
using the N1 standard
32 instance type.

00:36:34.050 --> 00:36:36.870
We created two queue groups
and our render technicians

00:36:36.870 --> 00:36:38.820
simply tagged the
high memory jobs

00:36:38.820 --> 00:36:41.000
to go to the appropriate group.

00:36:41.000 --> 00:36:44.640
But you might be thinking, why
not run a high man instance?

00:36:44.640 --> 00:36:46.300
Well we looked at
that option and we

00:36:46.300 --> 00:36:48.490
determine while threading
our application,

00:36:48.490 --> 00:36:56.080
over 32 vCPUs versus 16 was
about 17% less efficient.

00:36:56.080 --> 00:37:01.360
The cost for a high
[? mount ?] 16 VCP instance

00:37:01.360 --> 00:37:07.550
was 25% more than a 32
vCPU standard instance.

00:37:07.550 --> 00:37:10.270
And I think that's what's really
great about the Google Cloud--

00:37:10.270 --> 00:37:14.430
there's always an option
to meet your specific need.

00:37:14.430 --> 00:37:18.120
Even though we only had a few
weeks between cloud bursting

00:37:18.120 --> 00:37:21.150
projects, we were able to
make a few improvements

00:37:21.150 --> 00:37:23.340
since "Hotel T".

00:37:23.340 --> 00:37:26.220
First, we introduced the
concept of unit testing.

00:37:26.220 --> 00:37:29.970
It mandated a full scale
test for any major change

00:37:29.970 --> 00:37:31.890
to the system.

00:37:31.890 --> 00:37:34.580
We also learned
the hard way to not

00:37:34.580 --> 00:37:37.700
assume that nothing had
changed between spinning down

00:37:37.700 --> 00:37:41.090
the environment and
spinning it back up again.

00:37:41.090 --> 00:37:43.940
Just because it worked
before, doesn't mean it's

00:37:43.940 --> 00:37:44.960
going to work again.

00:37:44.960 --> 00:37:48.110
So you really do need
to continually test.

00:37:48.110 --> 00:37:50.530
Second, we refined
our queueing logic

00:37:50.530 --> 00:37:53.870
to more quickly
onboard cloud machines.

00:37:53.870 --> 00:37:57.770
The lag between instant
spin up and queue onboarding

00:37:57.770 --> 00:38:02.280
for prior projects really ate
into our overall efficiency.

00:38:02.280 --> 00:38:05.900
Now, to be fair,
on those projects,

00:38:05.900 --> 00:38:09.170
efficiency was not
one of our main goals.

00:38:09.170 --> 00:38:12.110
Success then was
really simply measured

00:38:12.110 --> 00:38:15.520
by launching an instance,
getting it in the queue,

00:38:15.520 --> 00:38:17.930
computing a frame,
and shutting it down.

00:38:17.930 --> 00:38:20.660
If we could do that,
that was success.

00:38:20.660 --> 00:38:22.910
And lastly, as I
mentioned, we introduced

00:38:22.910 --> 00:38:24.380
two tiers of service--

00:38:24.380 --> 00:38:26.600
the standard and high memory--

00:38:26.600 --> 00:38:29.180
and created two queue
groups to dispatch jobs to.

00:38:31.950 --> 00:38:34.930
Now here's a cloud usage
chart for "Smallfoot".

00:38:34.930 --> 00:38:37.450
In contrast to
"Hotel Transylvania",

00:38:37.450 --> 00:38:39.370
there's much more
even utilization

00:38:39.370 --> 00:38:43.190
over a similar three day period.

00:38:43.190 --> 00:38:44.710
The queue is mostly
full, and that's

00:38:44.710 --> 00:38:48.310
because a portion of the
jobs tend to run much longer.

00:38:48.310 --> 00:38:51.310
We also believe the queue
is doing a much better job

00:38:51.310 --> 00:38:54.220
of dispatching new
work to instances

00:38:54.220 --> 00:38:57.640
that were nowhere near
the 24 hour time limit

00:38:57.640 --> 00:39:01.030
imposed on all PVMs.

00:39:01.030 --> 00:39:03.130
The 24 hour time
limit is something

00:39:03.130 --> 00:39:07.720
you have to manage in balance
with the risk of preemption.

00:39:07.720 --> 00:39:10.390
The longer an instance
runs, the less chance

00:39:10.390 --> 00:39:11.770
there is of preemption.

00:39:11.770 --> 00:39:15.430
So if your job runs for
a short period of time,

00:39:15.430 --> 00:39:18.250
it's in your interest to
keep using that instance up

00:39:18.250 --> 00:39:21.340
to the point where
the timer will expire.

00:39:21.340 --> 00:39:24.790
You need to feel this
out and act accordingly.

00:39:24.790 --> 00:39:28.810
Initially, we wanted to compute
a frame and kill the instance.

00:39:28.810 --> 00:39:30.970
It seemed simple
and straightforward

00:39:30.970 --> 00:39:33.160
and felt like it would be
the most efficient thing

00:39:33.160 --> 00:39:34.540
for us to do.

00:39:34.540 --> 00:39:39.200
But many of our jobs would
finish in under five minutes.

00:39:39.200 --> 00:39:41.290
So that proved to
be a poor strategy

00:39:41.290 --> 00:39:44.920
because we increased both
our overhead and preemption.

00:39:44.920 --> 00:39:47.860
Had our frame time's been,
say, 10 hours or more,

00:39:47.860 --> 00:39:50.440
it may not have been
such a bad strategy. ,

00:39:50.440 --> 00:39:52.990
Again you need to tailor your
approach to your specific

00:39:52.990 --> 00:39:55.620
application workload.

00:39:55.620 --> 00:39:57.880
Now because "Smallfoot's"
confidence in GCP

00:39:57.880 --> 00:40:00.880
grew over the duration of
the project, a week ago

00:40:00.880 --> 00:40:02.680
they doubled the
number of instances

00:40:02.680 --> 00:40:09.430
and now keep about 15,000 to
18,000 cores busy almost 24/7.

00:40:09.430 --> 00:40:11.680
When combined with
on-prem resources,

00:40:11.680 --> 00:40:14.230
the total number of cores
"Smallfoot" can keep busy

00:40:14.230 --> 00:40:16.720
is well over 50,000.

00:40:16.720 --> 00:40:21.310
When I last checked, they were
computing 600,000 frames a day.

00:40:21.310 --> 00:40:23.290
They'll deliver the
project sometime

00:40:23.290 --> 00:40:26.350
in the next three weeks.

00:40:26.350 --> 00:40:28.600
Now here's a break down of
where the costs have landed

00:40:28.600 --> 00:40:30.290
thus far on this project.

00:40:30.290 --> 00:40:33.400
It's not too dissimilar
from "Hotel TIII".

00:40:33.400 --> 00:40:35.560
By percentage of
total spend, there's

00:40:35.560 --> 00:40:37.960
slightly less network
and a little more cache

00:40:37.960 --> 00:40:39.190
in processing.

00:40:39.190 --> 00:40:42.610
"Hotel TIII rendered
about 40% more frames

00:40:42.610 --> 00:40:47.080
daily than does "Smallfoot" so
this seems reasonable to me.

00:40:49.820 --> 00:40:51.740
All right, so I'll
wrap up by sharing

00:40:51.740 --> 00:40:57.730
what the next steps are that
we intend to take with cloud.

00:40:57.730 --> 00:41:01.460
There are five areas I see us
focusing on in the near term.

00:41:01.460 --> 00:41:04.720
The first one, and perhaps
even the most important,

00:41:04.720 --> 00:41:06.700
is around socialization.

00:41:06.700 --> 00:41:09.190
Stakeholders at all
levels within the company

00:41:09.190 --> 00:41:11.470
need to understand
what the cloud is,

00:41:11.470 --> 00:41:14.500
the benefits it brings to
potential business enablers,

00:41:14.500 --> 00:41:18.020
and how it can be used to
change almost everything we do.

00:41:18.020 --> 00:41:19.990
In the three projects
I discussed, at first

00:41:19.990 --> 00:41:23.570
each team was very
skeptical about using cloud.

00:41:23.570 --> 00:41:27.250
And by the end of their projects
all had become evangelists.

00:41:27.250 --> 00:41:29.800
Our senior management,
producers, supervisors,

00:41:29.800 --> 00:41:33.820
artists, and developers
all need to understand

00:41:33.820 --> 00:41:36.670
how the responsible
use of cloud computing

00:41:36.670 --> 00:41:40.130
can help the business reach
its goals quickly, efficiently,

00:41:40.130 --> 00:41:43.140
and without any
sacrifice to quality.

00:41:43.140 --> 00:41:46.350
Socializing cloud within the
company is very important

00:41:46.350 --> 00:41:49.810
and something will continue to
put a lot of effort towards.

00:41:49.810 --> 00:41:52.360
It's also important to socialize
cloud within the larger

00:41:52.360 --> 00:41:55.600
industry so that even our
clients are educated on what

00:41:55.600 --> 00:41:58.270
the benefits of using it are.

00:41:58.270 --> 00:42:00.760
Efficiency-- we're
pretty efficient once we

00:42:00.760 --> 00:42:04.170
get an instance managed
by our queuing system.

00:42:04.170 --> 00:42:07.150
But we have some work
to do on the logic

00:42:07.150 --> 00:42:10.000
that we use to determine whether
shutting the instance down

00:42:10.000 --> 00:42:13.515
or dispatch another frame
when a job completes.

00:42:13.515 --> 00:42:14.890
We're also not as
efficient as we

00:42:14.890 --> 00:42:17.430
could be managing the full
lifecycle of the instance,

00:42:17.430 --> 00:42:20.670
so that's another area
for us to focus on.

00:42:20.670 --> 00:42:23.580
Orchestration-- we'd like
to orchestrate the render

00:42:23.580 --> 00:42:26.440
environment directly
from the queuing system.

00:42:26.440 --> 00:42:29.940
After all, the queuing system is
the thing dispatching the work

00:42:29.940 --> 00:42:31.380
to these machines.

00:42:31.380 --> 00:42:33.330
And we figured doing
that would allow

00:42:33.330 --> 00:42:38.810
us to pick up as much as 10%
to 15% in overall efficiency.

00:42:38.810 --> 00:42:42.080
Monitoring and logging--
while we do extensive logging

00:42:42.080 --> 00:42:45.500
and statistics collection
on the jobs we run in Q3,

00:42:45.500 --> 00:42:47.510
we want to better
understand the full picture

00:42:47.510 --> 00:42:49.310
so that we'll
incorporate more of what

00:42:49.310 --> 00:42:52.460
we logged to BigQuery
and Stackdriver

00:42:52.460 --> 00:42:54.830
into our daily reporting.

00:42:54.830 --> 00:42:58.220
I see these stats feeding
into some custom dashboard

00:42:58.220 --> 00:43:02.480
that we can specifically
tailor to each viewer.

00:43:02.480 --> 00:43:03.650
And then there's reach.

00:43:03.650 --> 00:43:05.450
We'd like to extend
our rendering reach

00:43:05.450 --> 00:43:10.160
beyond US West, which will
involve pushing some pipeline

00:43:10.160 --> 00:43:12.260
servers into the
cloud to mitigate

00:43:12.260 --> 00:43:14.630
the additional network latency.

00:43:14.630 --> 00:43:17.900
Running multiple zones
reduces risk, especially

00:43:17.900 --> 00:43:22.250
as our resource requirements
continue to grow.

00:43:22.250 --> 00:43:24.080
A little further out,
we see other areas

00:43:24.080 --> 00:43:27.050
we can leverage cloud to
enhance and grow our business.

00:43:27.050 --> 00:43:29.120
And Todd was sort
of alluding to this

00:43:29.120 --> 00:43:30.880
towards the end of his talk.

00:43:30.880 --> 00:43:33.510
First is perhaps
the most obvious,

00:43:33.510 --> 00:43:36.230
which would be to
expand to a full studio

00:43:36.230 --> 00:43:38.060
pipeline in the cloud.

00:43:38.060 --> 00:43:41.900
And I think there's four kind
of key parts to this step.

00:43:41.900 --> 00:43:45.580
First is to expand our rendering
to include more types of jobs.

00:43:45.580 --> 00:43:47.780
Effect simulations
and compositing

00:43:47.780 --> 00:43:50.270
are two big areas for us.

00:43:50.270 --> 00:43:54.380
Cloud workstations to accelerate
workflows and enable new office

00:43:54.380 --> 00:43:58.400
locations and/or mitigate
the inevitable surges

00:43:58.400 --> 00:44:02.260
in labor often required
to complete projects.

00:44:02.260 --> 00:44:04.580
Dialinging in performance
on workstations

00:44:04.580 --> 00:44:07.490
will allow us also to
pursue more real time

00:44:07.490 --> 00:44:09.500
workflows at the desktop.

00:44:09.500 --> 00:44:12.380
Storage-- we use cloud files
store and other third party

00:44:12.380 --> 00:44:14.990
storage options to
migrate our data

00:44:14.990 --> 00:44:17.750
and extend our pipeline
into the cloud.

00:44:17.750 --> 00:44:21.050
Our pipeline is NFS based,
and our applications

00:44:21.050 --> 00:44:23.620
require a POSIX
compliant file system.

00:44:23.620 --> 00:44:26.990
And we don't expect that
to change anytime soon.

00:44:26.990 --> 00:44:28.790
Lastly, there is our pipeline.

00:44:28.790 --> 00:44:31.130
We'll migrate our middle
tier and pipeline servers

00:44:31.130 --> 00:44:33.410
into the cloud and
re-write applications

00:44:33.410 --> 00:44:37.920
to scale up and down, on-demand,
or run its micro services.

00:44:37.920 --> 00:44:40.640
And a lot of these
applications have been written

00:44:40.640 --> 00:44:42.470
and assume that
the machine is just

00:44:42.470 --> 00:44:47.480
on 24/7 so it's difficult to
just swing these things over

00:44:47.480 --> 00:44:49.080
to cloud.

00:44:49.080 --> 00:44:51.620
Now, if we can enable a
full studio in the cloud,

00:44:51.620 --> 00:44:54.350
we can add additional
office locations

00:44:54.350 --> 00:44:58.130
when our clients and our
projects require us to do so.

00:44:58.130 --> 00:45:00.290
We can truly become
a global company

00:45:00.290 --> 00:45:02.150
and leveraging
Google's global network

00:45:02.150 --> 00:45:05.730
will allow us to do it quickly
and on terms that we set.

00:45:05.730 --> 00:45:08.050
It's really important.

00:45:08.050 --> 00:45:11.150
There are also great
solutions to explore

00:45:11.150 --> 00:45:14.330
around backup, disaster
recovery, and business

00:45:14.330 --> 00:45:15.410
continuance.

00:45:15.410 --> 00:45:17.270
Increasingly, with
the sheer amount

00:45:17.270 --> 00:45:20.630
of data that we generate,
traditional tape backup

00:45:20.630 --> 00:45:22.850
no longer meets our needs.

00:45:22.850 --> 00:45:27.230
God forbid we lose a file
system on one of our servers,

00:45:27.230 --> 00:45:29.300
our only recourse is
to try and piece it

00:45:29.300 --> 00:45:32.120
back together again
from probably hundreds

00:45:32.120 --> 00:45:33.740
and hundreds of tapes.

00:45:33.740 --> 00:45:37.130
It's just not going
to happen in time.

00:45:37.130 --> 00:45:39.880
And finally, the Google
beyond corp tools

00:45:39.880 --> 00:45:41.980
that are available
today allow us

00:45:41.980 --> 00:45:45.070
to set up cloud based
identity management.

00:45:45.070 --> 00:45:48.670
These can be leveraged in
conjunction with cloud KMS

00:45:48.670 --> 00:45:51.610
to secure the data we
move into the cloud

00:45:51.610 --> 00:45:54.490
and implement multi-factor
authentication

00:45:54.490 --> 00:45:57.850
on any web based application
using the identity aware

00:45:57.850 --> 00:45:59.800
proxy service.

00:45:59.800 --> 00:46:03.700
We use the IAP today
to extend flics,

00:46:03.700 --> 00:46:05.740
which is a collaborative
story development

00:46:05.740 --> 00:46:10.030
application from the Foundry, to
remote Sony Pictures animation

00:46:10.030 --> 00:46:11.110
users.

00:46:11.110 --> 00:46:13.420
It works great and
it mitigates the need

00:46:13.420 --> 00:46:19.180
for cumbersome and complicated
VPM based solutions.

00:46:19.180 --> 00:46:21.430
So that's all I've
got for you guys.

00:46:21.430 --> 00:46:22.450
I want to thank you.

00:46:22.450 --> 00:46:25.150
I wish you luck in
using the Google Cloud.

00:46:25.150 --> 00:46:26.950
If you have any
questions, please

00:46:26.950 --> 00:46:30.270
feel free to reach out to
me at this email address.

00:46:30.270 --> 00:46:33.180
And I think we've got a
few minutes for questions

00:46:33.180 --> 00:46:36.400
if anyone wants to come
up to the microphone

00:46:36.400 --> 00:46:38.420
so they can get the audio.

00:46:41.870 --> 00:46:42.980
SPEAKER: OK, thank you.

00:46:42.980 --> 00:46:43.710
Thanks Steve.

00:46:43.710 --> 00:46:45.340
STEVE KOWALSKI: Yeah, thank you.

00:46:45.340 --> 00:46:48.090
[MUSIC PLAYING]

